    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d43">

                <pb facs="0061" n="739" sameAs="frus:frus1863p2/pg739"/>

                <head>Mr. Dayton to Mr. Seward</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 307.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1863-05-15"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >May</hi> 15, 1863.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your despatches, Nos. 336, 337, and 338,
                    have been duly received. I yesterday called upon Mr. Drouyn de l’Huys, and
                    communicated to him the views expressed in No. 336, in reference to the action
                    and purposes of the government of the United States as to the issue of letters
                    of marque and the kind terms in which you wrote of the manner in which the
                    French government had heretofore carried out the doctrines of neutrality in
                    their ports. He appeared to be pleased with these matters, but made no
                    additional remarks on these subjects which require to be reported.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. L. DAYTON.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William</hi> H. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Seward</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of State,
                        &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
